<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calatayud</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calatayud is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalon">Jalón</a> river in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It grew from the Muslim fortress of Qal'at Ayyub, founded in the 8th century near the ruins of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbilis">Bilbilis</a>, birthplace of the poet Martial. Briefly the seat of a Hudid principality in the 11th century, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> in 1120 and is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> churches and towers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calatayud.jpg" alt="Calatayud" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_Calatayud_desde_la_iglesia_de_La_Pe%C3%B1a,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2012-08-24,_DD_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
